# Import prompts

# Prompt
* export function fakePathDotResolve(...paths: string[]): string
This function should first try to detect if paths are windows paths or linux paths.
How to detect:
 - If one of the parts which is not the first part is absolute, then it is returned as path
 with the exception case when first part is a windows absolute path with drive letter and 
 the other absolute part is a unix absolute path, then we take drive letter from the first path, 
 and the rest path from the last absolute path;
 - Take the first part of the path;
 - If it contains a starting path with a drive letter, e.g., `D:\\folder`, it is windows
 - If it contains the windows separator `\\`, it is windows
 - If it contains the Linux separator, it is linux
 - If the part contains no signs of linux/windows path, it is not detected
 - If the first parts is not detected (windows or linux), then go to the second part
 - If no part is detected, then detect path separator according to current operating
 system (windows or not)
 - If detected, then merge all parts of separating by either linux or windows path separator
 respectively
 - return the merged result

 * Guidelines:
 To simplify algorithm, please explicitly process the first two parts of the path.
 Then, if there are more than two paths, run the function recursively setting as the first
 element the result of the resolve and then other parts, until there are only two paths left.